{
 "agent": "claude-code",
 "answer": {
  "candidates": [
   {
    "id": "C1",
    "quote": "the nature of the language to be designed (non-strict, purely functional)",
    "quote_row": "written:2007-06-09_01-introduction:nature-non-strict-purely-functional",
    "rows": [
     "written:2007-06-09_01-introduction:nature-non-strict-purely-functional",
     "written:2007-06-09_02-the-genesis-of-haskell:laziness-as-a-drug",
     "written:2007-06-09_02-the-genesis-of-haskell:lazy-evaluation-invented-three-times",
     "written:2007-06-09_02-the-genesis-of-haskell:lazy-lists-emulate-behaviours",
     "written:2007-06-09_07-monads-and-input-output:lazy-evaluation-enables-streams",
     "written:2007-06-09_10-profiling-and-debugging:laziness-abstracts-object-lifetimes",
     "written:2007-06-09_10-profiling-and-debugging:lazy-evaluation-hides-operational-aspects",
     "written:2007-06-09_10-profiling-and-debugging:space-sensitivity-two-edged"
    ],
    "statement": "L is non-strict, purely functional with lazy (call-by-need) evaluation as its defining core, embracing both the power (infinite structures, elegant combinators) and the cost (unpredictable space and evaluation order) that laziness brings.",
    "time_local": "2007-06-09 00:00:00",
    "who": "Paul Hudak, John Hughes, Simon Peyton Jones, Philip Wadler"
   },
   {
    "id": "C2",
    "quote": "The Haskell Committee was resolute in its decision to keep the language pure—meaning no side effects— so the design of the I/O system was an important issue.",
    "quote_row": "written:2007-06-09_07-monads-and-input-output:purity-drove-io-design",
    "rows": [
     "written:2007-06-09_07-monads-and-input-output:purity-drove-io-design",
     "written:2007-06-09_07-monads-and-input-output:monadic-io-key-idea",
     "written:2007-06-09_07-monads-and-input-output:monad-definition",
     "written:2007-06-09_07-monads-and-input-output:being-explicit-about-effects",
     "written:2007-06-09_13-conclusion:longterm-bet-on-principled-control-of-effects",
     "written:2007-06-09_13-conclusion:haskell-contributed-through-purity-and-critical-mass",
     "written:2007-06-09_07-monads-and-input-output:exceptions-recovered-via-io",
     "written:2007-06-09_07-monads-and-input-output:runST-encapsulates-imperative"
    ],
    "statement": "Purity, meaning no side effects, is held to firmly, which makes controlled handling of effects (I/O, state, exceptions) through monads the central design achievement and L's conscious long-term bet.",
    "time_local": "2007-06-09 00:00:00",
    "who": "Paul Hudak, John Hughes, Simon Peyton Jones, Philip Wadler"
   },
   {
    "id": "C3",
    "quote": "Aside from laziness, type classes are undoubtedly Haskell's most distinctive feature.",
    "quote_row": "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:type-classes-as-distinctive-feature",
    "rows": [
     "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:type-classes-as-distinctive-feature",
     "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:type-classes-origin-small-problem",
     "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:type-class-basic-idea",
     "written:2007-06-09_02-the-genesis-of-haskell:constructor-classes",
     "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:constructor-classes-higher-kinded",
     "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:higher-kinded-as-elegant-generalisation",
     "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:type-classes-serendipity",
     "written:2007-06-09_01-introduction:type-classes-as-distinctive"
    ],
    "statement": "Type classes, with their generalisations to higher kinds (constructor classes), multi-parameter classes, and functional dependencies, are L's most distinctive feature after laziness and the principled solution to overloading.",
    "time_local": "2007-06-09 00:00:00",
    "who": "Paul Hudak, John Hughes, Simon Peyton Jones, Philip Wadler"
   },
   {
    "id": "C4",
    "quote": "Miranda added strong polymorphic typing and type inference, ideas that had proven very successful in ML.",
    "quote_row": "written:2007-06-09_02-the-genesis-of-haskell:miranda-polymorphic-typing",
    "rows": [
     "written:2007-06-09_02-the-genesis-of-haskell:miranda-polymorphic-typing",
     "written:2007-06-09_05-data-types-and-pattern-matching:capitalization-rule-extended-to-types",
     "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:anarchic-development",
     "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:haskell-as-type-system-laboratory",
     "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:why-haskell-friendly-to-innovation-technical",
     "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:higher-rank-types",
     "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:polymorphic-recursion",
     "written:2007-06-09_06-haskell-as-a-type-system-laboratory:generalised-algebraic-data-types"
    ],
    "statement": "L has strong static polymorphic typing with Hindley-Milner inference, inherited from ML via Miranda and extended anarchically into a laboratory for type-system research.",
    "time_local": "2007-06-09 00:00:00",
    "who": "Paul Hudak, John Hughes, Simon Peyton Jones, Philip Wadler"
   },
   {
    "id": "C5",
    "quote": "Hope, the language that introduced algebraic data types",
    "quote_row": "written:2007-06-09_02-the-genesis-of-haskell:hope-algebraic-data-types",
    "rows": [
     "written:2007-06-09_02-the-genesis-of-haskell:hope-algebraic-data-types",
     "written:2007-06-09_05-data-types-and-pattern-matching:sum-of-products-from-miranda",
     "written:2007-06-09_05-data-types-and-pattern-matching:data-constructors-two-roles",
     "written:2007-06-09_05-data-types-and-pattern-matching:data-types-and-pattern-matching-fundamental",
     "written:2007-06-09_05-data-types-and-pattern-matching:pattern-matching-increases-readability",
     "written:2007-06-09_05-data-types-and-pattern-matching:top-to-bottom-left-to-right-matching",
     "written:2007-06-09_02-the-genesis-of-haskell:adt-extensions",
     "written:2007-06-09_05-data-types-and-pattern-matching:basic-algebraic-types-straightforward-rest-interesting"
    ],
    "statement": "Algebraic data types defined as a sum of products, together with top-to-bottom left-to-right pattern matching, are fundamental to the language and its readability.",
    "time_local": "2007-06-09 00:00:00",
    "who": "Paul Hudak, John Hughes, Simon Peyton Jones, Philip Wadler"
   },
   {
    "id": "C6",
    "quote": "Despite this, the Haskell Committee worked very hard...on designing (and arguing about) the syntax of Haskell.",
    "quote_row": "written:2007-06-09_04-syntax:syntax-taken-seriously-despite-dismissal",
    "rows": [
     "written:2007-06-09_04-syntax:syntax-taken-seriously-despite-dismissal",
     "written:2007-06-09_04-syntax:syntax-as-personal-user-interface",
     "written:2007-06-09_04-syntax:layout-avoids-clutter",
     "written:2007-06-09_04-syntax:infix-operators-like-mathematics",
     "written:2007-06-09_04-syntax:application-by-juxtaposition",
     "written:2007-06-09_04-syntax:sections",
     "written:2007-06-09_04-syntax:few-reserved-keywords",
     "written:2007-06-09_04-syntax:haskell-is-a-pretty-language"
    ],
    "statement": "Syntax was treated as the user interface and worked very hard: layout/offside rule, infix operators and sections, currying by juxtaposition, few keywords, aiming for programs that look like mathematics and read nicely.",
    "time_local": "2007-06-09 00:00:00",
    "who": "Paul Hudak, John Hughes, Simon Peyton Jones, Philip Wadler"
   },
   {
    "id": "C7",
    "quote": "It was decided that a committee should be formed to design such a language",
    "quote_row": "written:2007-06-09_01-introduction:design-by-committee",
    "rows": [
     "written:2007-06-09_01-introduction:design-by-committee",
     "written:2007-06-09_02-the-genesis-of-haskell:conservative-consensus-intent",
     "written:2007-06-09_01-introduction:motivation-common-language",
     "written:2007-06-09_01-introduction:lack-of-common-language-hampers-use",
     "written:2007-06-09_02-the-genesis-of-haskell:haskell98-commitment-to-stability",
     "written:2007-06-09_12-the-impact-of-haskell:haskell98-stability-step",
     "written:2007-06-09_01-introduction:community-as-cause-and-indicator-of-success",
     "written:2007-06-09_02-the-genesis-of-haskell:tower-of-babel-common-language"
    ],
    "statement": "L was built by committee as a conservative common standard embodying consensus (ideally tried-and-true ideas) to unite the scattered lazy-functional community, with stability (L 98) and a growing community taken as both goal and measure of success.",
    "time_local": "2007-06-09 00:00:00",
    "who": "Paul Hudak, John Hughes, Simon Peyton Jones, Philip Wadler"
   }
  ]
 },
